v9ai/ai-engineer-roadmap:content/llm-serving.md: Traditional ML serving uses **static batching**: collect N requests, process them as a batch, return all results. This works terribly for LLM inference because requests have wildly different output lengths. In a static batch, all requests must wait for the longest one to finish, wasting GPU cycles on padding.
v9ai/ai-engineer-roadmap:content/llm-serving.md: The Orca paper (Yu et al., 2022, "Orca: A Distributed Serving System for Transformer-Based Generative Models") introduced **continuous batching** (also called **iteration-level scheduling**). The key insight: instead of batching at the request level, batch at the iteration (token) level.
jaipack17/write-ups:LLM Serving/Overview of vLLM, Orca, SGLang, vAttention.md: KVCache grows and shrinks dynamically, and for a large number of requests, it needs to be managed properly. LLM inference is **memory bound**.
jaipack17/write-ups:LLM Serving/Overview of vLLM, Orca, SGLang, vAttention.md: ## Paper-1: Orca Serving System (2022)
jaipack17/write-ups:LLM Serving/Overview of vLLM, Orca, SGLang, vAttention.md: Orca is a distributed serving system that helps scale and serve LLMs at high speed. It introduces **iteration-level scheduling** and **selective batching** which are essential improvements in the way requests are handled before models process them.
